WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.320
- Welcome back everybody,

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:03.660
We've got Chiara Aurelia,
Mila Kunis with us.

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:04.497
Give it up.

00:00:04.497 --> 00:00:06.150
(clapping and cheering)

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:08.137
- Together, they star in
the new Netflix movie,

00:00:08.137 --> 00:00:09.510
"Luckiest Girl Alive".

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:11.010
It starts streaming tomorrow.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.830
Also, joining us now is the
woman who wrote the book,

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:16.170
and was an executive producer
on the film, as well.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:18.480
Please welcome "New York
Times" bestselling author,

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:19.734
Jessica Knoll.

00:00:19.734 --> 00:00:22.740
(clapping and cheering)

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.200
- It's amazing to me 'cause
your name sounds like

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:26.516
a character in a book.

00:00:26.516 --> 00:00:27.990
- We were just having a little bit

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:29.430
of a name conversation here.

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:31.290
- Yeah, but your name
sounds like the, like,

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:33.030
lead character in a novel.

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:34.017
Jessica Knoll.
- I'll take it, thank you.

00:00:34.017 --> 00:00:37.290
So, this book and the movie
are based on your real life,

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:41.130
which, while watching, was
a real struggle for me,

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:42.600
and I think most people when they watch it

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.030
'cause it's very intense,
and to be that vulnerable

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:48.780
and open is a very next level in your step

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:49.740
of progressing as a human.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:50.833
- Yeah.

00:00:50.833 --> 00:00:52.320
- 'Cause a lot of people,
and I know, honestly,

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.140
far too many people that
have had trauma happen.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:55.973
- [Jessica] Yeah.

00:00:55.973 --> 00:00:58.200
- And it- they don't- they're
still like 40 years old

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.093
and haven't been able to get through it.

00:01:01.093 --> 00:01:01.926
- Yeah.

00:01:01.926 --> 00:01:02.759
- And so it's incredible.

00:01:02.759 --> 00:01:04.650
So, I'm just saying thank
you for all those people,

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:07.740
but how did you find the
courage to tell that story

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:09.330
or own that story in life?

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:11.850
- I mean, I don't think at the time

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:14.070
that I sat down and
began telling this story,

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:16.747
which was 2013 at my kitchen table

00:01:16.747 --> 00:01:18.600
at my apartment in New York City.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:19.433
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:19.433 --> 00:01:21.390
I don't even think I was thinking beyond

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:22.860
what I was doing in that moment.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:24.570
And I was doing it for me.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:28.110
I had finally found a
way to put on the page

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.840
this thing that had haunted
me for most of my life,

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.030
and I did it under the cover

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:34.590
and the protection of fiction.

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:36.030
Like, that was really important.

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:39.300
Like, I wasn't- I wasn't ready yet

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.790
and I was too frightened by my own-

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:43.920
by what had happened
to me when I was young.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.290
The way that it was mis-characterized.

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:47.123
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:47.123 --> 00:01:48.480
- And the way I was blamed for it.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:50.797
I was still so scarred
from that, that I was like,

00:01:50.797 --> 00:01:53.910
"I'm gonna put this in a work of fiction

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:56.190
and then I'll let the public decide".

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:59.790
If they call it rape,
if they call it assault,

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:04.380
then I'll know, finally, I'm
validated that I feel this way.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:07.230
And so, for the next year
after the book came out,

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:09.390
every time someone would say to me,

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:11.797
just in passing not even realizing, like,

00:02:11.797 --> 00:02:14.400
"oh that assault scene
was so difficult to read",

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.010
or like, "you rendered
it so realistically,

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:17.843
how did you do that?".

00:02:17.843 --> 00:02:19.980
I'm, like, you don't know
what you're giving me.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.380
Like, you're giving me the validation

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:25.074
that 15-year-old me sought and didn't get

00:02:25.074 --> 00:02:26.850
when she needed it most.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:28.530
- Yeah. Wow.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.180
- [Jessica] Yeah, it was
a really powerful thing.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:32.730
- Yeah. So you also wrote the screenplay,

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:33.840
which, Mila and I, we were talking about,

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.640
most people don't do, like,
most authors don't do.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:36.473
- No.

00:02:36.473 --> 00:02:38.760
- Did you really- I mean,
'cause I find I, if it were me,

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:41.370
I would find it hard to
let go of that character

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:42.510
in somebody else's hands.

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:46.590
- Yeah. Well at the point that
I- that the book was optioned

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:50.064
and we were even talking about
it being- becoming a movie,

00:02:50.064 --> 00:02:52.140
I still hadn't written
my essay where I came out

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.300
and said that this had happened to me.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:57.330
So I think I still felt
so wildly protective

00:02:57.330 --> 00:02:59.640
of this character and
this voice and this story.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:03.300
And the idea of someone else tackling it

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.790
and writing it made me crazed.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:07.770
Like, I couldn't stomach the thought.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:09.995
I was like, "I have to
be the one to do this"

00:03:09.995 --> 00:03:10.828
- [Kelly] Rightly so.

00:03:10.828 --> 00:03:11.760
Yeah. Yeah.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:14.414
- What conversations
do you hope this movie

00:03:14.414 --> 00:03:15.990
opens up for people?

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:17.610
Like what kind of dialogue?

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:20.340
- I mean, I woke up this
morning to a message

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:24.240
that my agent passed along to
me from a 57-year-old woman

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:27.900
who said that she picked
up my book at the airport,

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:28.807
and she was, like,

00:03:28.807 --> 00:03:31.590
"I don't even know if this
message will ever get to Jessica,

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:34.380
but like, you know,
something happened to me

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:38.077
when I was young", and she
shared it with me and she said,

00:03:38.077 --> 00:03:43.077
"even if you never see this,
just putting this on the page

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:46.140
has been a therapy for me I
didn't know that I needed".

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:47.370
'Cause she hadn't talked about it

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:48.330
- And she's 57.

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:50.490
- Yeah, so I hope that's what happens

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:51.750
with the book and the essay.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:54.540
So, if it were to happen on a, you know,

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:55.860
such a broader scale,

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:58.830
which is what a platform
like Netflix can offer,

00:03:58.830 --> 00:03:59.663
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:59.663 --> 00:04:01.980
I, you know, I just think
that's such a great thing

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.290
for the world and for women at large.

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:05.340
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:06.330
- What about you guys?

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:08.670
Like, what conversations
do you hope kind of-

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:11.130
'cause you're a different
generation from us, right?

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:13.260
So like what do you hope this kind of-

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:15.240
'cause do you feel like your
friends are open enough?

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:16.560
Do you feel like your generation is open?

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:17.981
She's like "oh no".

00:04:17.981 --> 00:04:18.817
(laughing)

00:04:18.817 --> 00:04:20.430
I just mean, is it- is it hard

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:21.780
for you to talk to your friends?

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:23.430
Like about, like, serious matters?

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:25.260
It was harder for me whenever I was a kid.

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:26.130
- [Jessica] We didn't talk about it.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:26.963
- You didn't talk about anything.

00:04:26.963 --> 00:04:27.990
Everything was under the rug swept.

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:30.630
There's a whole line about it
in Alanis Morissette's song.

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:34.140
So yeah, I was like, she
knows, but is it easier?

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.030
I feel like your generation-
like even my nephew,

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:37.757
he's 20, the same age as you.

00:04:37.757 --> 00:04:40.350
He is way more progressive-

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:41.183
- [Jessica] Yeah.

00:04:41.183 --> 00:04:42.016
- Than I am.

00:04:42.016 --> 00:04:43.350
Like, I mean, and I'm pretty progressive,

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:44.970
but he's just more, I guess not,

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:46.459
progressive isn't the
word, more open-minded.

00:04:46.459 --> 00:04:47.292
- [Jessica] Or articulate.

00:04:47.292 --> 00:04:49.823
He's more open-minded, he's
okay with talking about things.

00:04:49.823 --> 00:04:51.300
- They have this language
that, like, I did not have-

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:52.347
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:52.347 --> 00:04:53.180
- At that age.

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:54.733
But we've kind of talked
about that, right?

00:04:54.733 --> 00:04:55.650
- We've talked about this a little bit.

00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:59.197
I mean, I think something
that's like really great about,

00:04:59.197 --> 00:05:01.020
you know, the time that we're in now

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:03.210
is people are becoming more aware.

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:04.680
They're willing to have conversations,

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:07.080
they're hoping, you know,
they're trying to learn.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:09.180
Does that mean that we're in the place

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:11.640
where everything is fixed
and we're, you know,

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:14.100
everyone knows exactly how
to handle the situation

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:15.690
and, you know, talks about it regularly

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:18.450
and is totally, you know,
educated about the situation?

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:20.790
I don't necessarily think so,

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:22.800
but I also think that people are trying,

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:25.920
which is what is the biggest
change that has happened.

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:27.690
- It makes me hopeful, your generation.

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:28.950
Like even talking with my nephew,

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:30.217
I spent the whole summer
with him and I was like,

00:05:30.217 --> 00:05:32.533
"I'm sorry, when did you grow up?".

00:05:32.533 --> 00:05:33.366
(laughing)

00:05:33.366 --> 00:05:36.840
- "Luckiest Girl Alive" start
streaming tomorrow on Netflix.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:38.910
And the book, also called
"Luckiest Girl Alive"

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:40.803
is available wherever books are sold.

